<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! Rust Toolchain ~ so the house can prove a fix rather than await it

Three of the four nakama forks ship as ''Rust crates that emit wasm'': `keyhive`, `subduction`, `onomancy`. Their npm packages arrive as ''prebuilt artefacts'' — consuming them means a fix reaches us only when upstream publishes. ''`66a6632 Fixes for CGKA access (#216)` stands in our keyhive fork's main and in no published release'': it limits transitive authority to the weakest link on the best path and stops Relay access transitively granting membership in a document's CGKA tree. A version pin cannot reach that. A ''build'' can.

The toolchain therefore serves as more than a convenience. It marks ''the difference between reading a fix and running it'', and between filing an issue and sending a patch that compiles.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/what-the-forks-pin`>>

!! What each fork pins ~ measured, not assumed

|!fork |!channel |!declared where |!components |
|`keyhive` |''1.90.0'' |`Cargo.toml` `rust-version` |— |
|`subduction` |''1.91.0'' |`rust-toolchain.toml` |`clippy`, `rustfmt` |
|`onomancy` |''1.91.0'' |`rust-toolchain.toml` |`clippy`, `rustfmt` |

''TWO CHANNELS, AND NO HARM IN IT.'' `rust-toolchain.toml` takes effect ''per directory'' — entering each fork selects its own channel with no manual switch and no global default to keep straight. Their own comments say the pin stays in lockstep with the workspace `rust-version` and the flake's `rustVersion`; ''bump all three or none''.

''All three carry a `flake.nix`.'' Upstream itself stands in the Nix environment.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-emitter`>>

!! The emitter ~ wasm-pack, three targets, one slim step

Each wasm crate builds the same way (`keyhive_wasm`, `subduction_wasm`, `automerge_subduction_wasm`, `sedimentree_wasm`):

```
wasm-pack build --out-dir pkg-node --target nodejs  --release
wasm-pack build --out-dir pkg      --target bundler --release
wasm-pack build --out-dir pkg-web  --target web     --release
node build_slim.js
```

''Three targets carry no redundancy'' — `pkg-node` serves the daemon, `pkg` serves a bundler, `pkg-web` serves a browser vessel, and the ''slim'' step emits the base64-embedded variant a page can load without a second fetch. This house already consumes all of those shapes.

⚠ ''`onomancy_wasm` declares NO build scripts.'' It builds through cargo/wasm-pack directly or through the flake. Do not read the empty script block as //not buildable//; read it as //no npm-side convenience//.

⚠ ''THE PIN-PAIR LAW REACHES HERE TOO.'' The grammar package already states it — //the CLI that generates and the runtime that loads never drift a minor apart//. Its analogue: the `wasm-pack` that builds and the `wasm-bindgen` version in the crate's `Cargo.lock` MUST agree. wasm-pack normally fetches a matching `wasm-bindgen-cli`; when it cannot, the failure reads as a mysterious ABI error rather than a version complaint.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/two-paths`>>

!! Two paths, and they serve different purposes

* ''RUSTUP — the working path.'' `rustup` plus the `wasm32-unknown-unknown` target plus `wasm-pack`. Each fork's `rust-toolchain.toml` selects its own channel on entry, so no one pins a channel by hand and nothing drifts globally. Lightest thing that builds.
* ''NIX — the donation-parity path.'' `nix develop` inside a fork stands ''the environment upstream's own CI stands''. A patch built there carries no environment-specific breakage into a pull request. Heavier to install; earns its cost at the moment we send code back rather than at the moment we read it.

''RECOMMENDED: rustup to work, nix to donate.'' The house machine carries neither (`rustc`, `cargo`, `wasm-pack`, `nix` all absent), so this section holds a standing plan rather than a description.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-divergence-law`>>

!! The law ~ a locally-built wasm declares a FLAG-DAY

''A wasm we build ourselves that differs in behaviour from the published package declares a flag-day against our own mesh.'' This reads #213 back at us: BeeKEM replacing path ratcheting with independently sampled secrets meant alpha.6 and alpha.8 peers could not key with each other, and a locally-built keyhive carrying #216 would do exactly that against a vessel running published alpha.8 — ''#216 changes authorization VERDICTS, so a patched and an unpatched node disagree about who sits in a CGKA tree''. A split-brain, not a desync, and nastier for it.

So, MUST:

* ''Every vessel in a mesh runs the SAME build.'' Not the same version string — the same artefact. A version string proves nothing when one side built its own.
* ''A local build carries its provenance.'' The fork's commit sha, the channel, the wasm-pack version, and the digest of the emitted `.wasm`, recorded where a vessel can read them. This house already binds a built blob's sha256 into `hearthTrueName` for TiddlyWiki5; the same instinct applies.
* ''Consuming published npm stays the DEFAULT'' until we can reproduce and record a build. Standing a toolchain buys the ability to build; it does not oblige us to ship what we build.
* ''The house consumes Ink & Switch libraries from npm, and a library becomes a source-built workspace member ONLY when it enters the engine'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-11). Entering the engine costs a pinned Rust/WASM stage in the container image and in CI, and the built bytes then bind into the engine's content address, so a re-pin of that library counts as an epoch act, as TiddlyWiki5's already does. A reading copy's re-pin changes nothing that runs.
* ''A patch we intend to donate gets proven in the fork FIRST'' — upstream's tests, upstream's environment — before it reaches a vessel here. A fix that only passes in our tree makes a fork, not a contribution.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-order`>>

!! The order

# ''Stand rustup + `wasm32-unknown-unknown` + wasm-pack.'' Verify by building ONE crate — `keyhive_wasm --target nodejs` — and reading the emitted `.wasm` size against the published `@keyhive/keyhive` artefact (2,289,235 bytes at alpha.8). A wildly different size gives the first signal that the build differs from the published build.
# ''Run the fork's OWN tests before trusting anything built from it'': `cargo test` in each, and the wasm-bindgen tests where they stand. A green local build over a red upstream suite proves the compiler works and nothing else.
# ''Reproduce alpha.8 from the tag before building main.'' If we cannot rebuild the artefact we already run, we cannot claim a build carrying #216 differs only by #216.
# ''Then, and only then, build main'' and measure what #216 changes in behaviour against our own cap-tier and face-join suites.
# ''Nix when the first patch stands ready to send'', not before.

⚠ Machine note: this stands under ''WSL2''. `rustup` works; a Nix install there wants the daemon-less single-user mode or a systemd-enabled distro, and that choice calls for its own small ceremony.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/open-loops`>>

!! Open loops ~ deferred, not dropped

''Held here because the same breath that approved the toolchain deferred them.'' None waits on the toolchain; each waits on a hand.

!!! The testing architecture (scouted 2026-09-02, plan in hand)

# ''ONE SERIALIZATION, BOTH PLACES — CLOSED.'' Root `package.json` reads `pnpm -r --workspace-concurrency=1 test`, and CI's `test` job runs ''`pnpm test`'' — the root script itself, so the serialization travels rather than getting re-spelled. The law rides in the workflow's own comment: packages share a build tree and a rendezvous directory, so a parallel run ''measures a different repository than the one an operator measures locally'', and its flakes stay unreproducible by construction. The standing invariant: CI invokes the ROOT SCRIPT, never a hand-copied command.
# ''ONE RUNNER STANDS UNINVOKED, and two ride CI. Measured, and here follows how to re-derive each'':
#* ''`witness-all` RUNS in CI, under its own job.'' `.github/workflows/ci.yml` seats a `witnesses` job that calls `tools/witness-all.sh` and then ''asserts the roll call'' — it greps the log for `held out of this run` and fails if the fleet's own naming of its hold-outs goes silent. Re-derive: `grep -n "witness-all" .github/workflows/ci.yml`.
#* ''The fleet counts 34 files and RUNS 29.'' `witness-all.sh` globs `tools/*witness*.sh` rather than keeping a hand list, so a new witness enrols by existing. `ls tools/*witness*.sh | wc -l` reads ''34''; `HELD_OUT` declares six names with a reason each, and ''five of them match the glob'' (civic · crossing · herm-mesh · browser-weld · witness-all itself) — leaving ''29 that run''. The sixth, `mesh-scenarios`, carries no `witness` in its filename and so sits outside the glob entirely; the script's second loop reports it anyway, because a hold-out that stops naming itself has become a silent skip.
#* ''`gate:fold-ts` HAS an invoker.'' The `grammar-artifact` job runs it as its own step (//fold + parity gates (ts host)//) beside `gate:fold` for the py host, deliberately unchained so a red in one host cannot hide a red in the other. Re-derive: `grep -n "gate:fold" .github/workflows/ci.yml`.
#* ⚠ ''The SENSORIUM python suite runs under no CI step.'' `pyproject.toml` `testpaths` names two trees; collect them and the split reads ''1016 tests'' total — `packages/tree-sitter-memetic-wikitext/host-py` contributes ''27'' and rides CI through `gate:fold` (`python -m pytest -q`), while `packages/lararium-sensorium/scripts` contributes ''989 across 96 files'' and no workflow step reaches them. Re-derive: `~/.venv/bin/python3 -m pytest --collect-only -q <path>`, once per testpath. ⚠ Expect red when lit: wire first, triage as its own stage, and ''never delete a red to make a stage green''.
# ''THE MOVE'' — `tests/` to `packages/lararium-tests/`. The trap does NOT lie in `pnpm -r test` picking it up: `pnpm -r` recurses over ''workspace members'', and `tests` already counts as one, so the move changes its path and not its membership. ''The script name `test:e2e` makes the only fence'' — keep it, and say so on the manifest. ⚠ The REAL trap lies in `typecheck-witness.sh`, which iterates `packages/*/` and ''silently skips any directory without a tsconfig''. The harness has none, so post-move its 218 lines vanish from the witness ''at the very address the witness claims to sweep''. Land a `tsconfig.typecheck.json` with the move.
# ''D2 — `invokeLocal` falls back to the operator's home vessel'' (`local-connector.ts`: `dataDir ?? larDataDir()`). A null dataDir reaches a live vessel. It made `herm-floor` R4 a FALSE GREEN and means ''R5's failure gives no evidence about the lift''. It should refuse, never default.
# ''D3 — the meme projector stamps a `bag` key the corpus never carries.'' `tw5-file-info.ts` excludes `bag` from `.tid`/`.json`/`.meta`; the memetic projection does not. Only 9 corpus files carry an authored `bag =`. Operator call, and it only became visible when a stale fence label stopped masking it.
# ''Seven socket failures'' await deriving the rendezvous path in the harness rather than hunting a filename.

!!! What must NOT collapse when that work fires

''The serialization'' — fix CI up to it, never the root script down. ''`lararium-node`'s two-project split and its THRASHERS list'' — one authoritative statement the roster reader imports, so the classes cannot drift between two statements. ''The four out-of-band witnesses'' — `mesh-coverage` stays permanently red BY DESIGN and reads claims never passes; three others need docker. ''Duplicate-named test files stand as WELDS, not duplicates'' — node≡browser agreement CONSTITUTES the island-isomorphism proof, and `seal-reserve`'s own weld pair (`seal-reserve-split.test.ts` against pure crypto in `lares-cli/src/seal-reserve.ts`, `seal-reserve.test.ts` against the on-disk ceremony in `lararium-node/src/seal-reserve-store.ts`) splits pure crypto from on-disk ceremony the same way. ''Source vs dist vs packed-plugin splits'' form the half-build instrument; collapsing one converts a weld into a tautology.

<<~/ahu>>

